-- reach.rofl — WHICH SERVICE CAN REACH WHICH, and what that costs to hold.
--
-- Two rules and no tricks. The graph is a layered call topology handed in by
-- demo.ts: sixteen services per layer, thirty layers, every service calling
-- every service in the layer below it. That shape is not exotic — it is what a
-- request path through a tiered system looks like when you stop pretending the
-- fan-out is one.
--
-- WHY THIS EXAMPLE EXISTS. `space_exhausted` and `budget_exhausted` are two
-- atoms in this kernel because they demand OPPOSITE repairs (src/reflect.ts:249)
-- and, until 2026-09-09, a caller could act on neither: the budget was a
-- parameter and the space was a constant nobody could reach. Now both are
-- settable and the difference has to be shown rather than asserted.
--
-- AND THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT "ONE IS BIGGER". `test/rule-shape.test.ts` gates a
-- rule whose premise shares no variable with what precedes it — a CROSS
-- PRODUCT, where the intermediate is large because the rule is wrong and the
-- repair is to reorder the body. Nothing here is that. `reaches(A, C) :-
-- reaches(A, B), calls(B, C).` joins on `B` at every step, and the answer
-- itself is quadratic: 111 360 pairs over 480 services, which is
-- 16 * 16 * 30 * 29 / 2 exactly and can be checked by hand. No ordering makes a
-- quadratic answer small. The only question left is whether you can hold it,
-- and that is what `space` is for.

edb(calls).

reaches(A, B) :- calls(A, B).
reaches(A, C) :- reaches(A, B), calls(B, C).

-- The two summaries the demo prints, so the transcript is the kernel's answer
-- and not the host's arithmetic.
serves(A)      :- reaches(A, _).
reachable(B)   :- reaches(_, B).
